WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.190
ALISON HILL: Welcome,
everyone, to the webinar

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:10.580
on Sharing on Short Notice,
where we'll show you

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:13.310
how to get your teaching
materials online

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:16.540
with R Markdown.

00:00:16.540 --> 00:00:18.040
So hello.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.880
We're really happy you
could join us today.

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.060
We know that everybody has
a lot of things going on,

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:27.280
so we appreciate you taking the
time to join us this afternoon.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.330
My name is Alison Hill,
and I'm a data scientist

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.523
and professional
educator at RStudio.

00:00:33.523 --> 00:00:35.840
DESIREE DE LEON:
And I'm Desiree,

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.940
and I interned with
Alison last summer

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:40.738
at RStudio on the
education team.

00:00:40.738 --> 00:00:42.280
ALISON HILL: And
even though we don't

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:43.720
have a video feed
in this webinar,

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.060
we promise we are real-life
humans who are also

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.130
stuck at home just
now like you all,

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:51.813
sitting in front
of our computers.

00:00:51.813 --> 00:00:53.980
And one thing that Desiree
and I both have in common

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.210
is that we ship a
lot of websites.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:00:58.320
I'm a developmental
psychologist.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.670
Desiree is a neuroscientist.

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:02.860
Neither of us is
a web developer,

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.780
but we both ship
a lot of websites

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.670
for teaching data science.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.670
And here are just a few of them.

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:12.970
If you type the short link
on the bottom of our slides,

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:20.290
it's rstd.io/sharing.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:22.360
You'll be able to see our
slides and follow along

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.180
in your browser.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:24.640
So you can see,
all of these links

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.040
are also applicable for you.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.630
Each of these sites
was built using

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.700
R Markdown with
different tools chosen

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:33.460
depending on what we needed.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:35.490
So that's us.

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.570
Who are you?

00:01:37.570 --> 00:01:40.440
We think you're an
educator, and you

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.580
have R Markdown files
that you use for teaching.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.505
But they may be stuck
on your computer

00:01:46.505 --> 00:01:47.880
and you need to
share them easily

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.610
with others without
filling up their inboxes.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.030
And that's because you
probably have a new classroom

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:55.830
space that looks like this.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:02:01.060
So sharing needs to
be done 100% online.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:03.550
And you might feel
like this right now.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.440
Everything seems to
need your attention,

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.120
but you cannot do everything.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.310
So with that in
mind, today we'd like

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.790
to give you a quick tour
of the R Markdown tools

00:02:14.790 --> 00:02:19.100
that you can use to get
your course materials online

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:20.420
on very short notice.

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.400
We'll show you how to
build a course website,

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.050
to help you teach
and reach learners

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.270
using R Markdown and friends.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:31.340
And our goal is to
give you a quick boost

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.140
to help you get your site up off
the ground with a sharable link

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:39.030
so that you can get
on with your teaching

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:43.350
and hopefully avoid
crashing in the process.

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:45.680
So why make a course
website at all?

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:48.900
I think of it as a way, first to
help me organize my own stuff,

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:50.940
to get all my piles
of teaching documents

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:52.860
into some kind of
order, and then

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:56.140
make some more reproducible
for myself and for others.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.950
But it's also a way to make my
teaching more resilient if I'm

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.260
organized and online,
I can be more nimble

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.460
when changes need to happen.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.030
And a side benefit of
all that is that it also

00:03:06.030 --> 00:03:07.032
makes it more shareable.

00:03:07.032 --> 00:03:08.490
So it makes it
easier for learners,

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:12.140
too, because they
see what I see.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:14.330
So we'll be building
websites with R Markdown.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:17.360
And because of that, we'll
start by talking about building

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.130
a single R Markdown file.

00:03:19.130 --> 00:03:20.505
So Desiree, you're up.

00:03:20.505 --> 00:03:21.630
DESIREE DE LEON: All right.

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:22.940
Thanks, Alison.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.600
So let's see.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:31.410
Jumping right in, what does
an R Markdown file look like?

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.510
So here's an R project
folder for a lab, let's say.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:39.170
And inside that folder, you'll
notice an R project file.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:44.030
And opening that file in
RStudio takes us here,

00:03:44.030 --> 00:03:46.710
where we can see our
single R Markdown file.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.550
And as I mentioned,
this is one for a lab

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:52.460
that's going to visualize data
from the Museum of Modern Art.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.200
So let's just quickly
walk through some

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.150
of the key parts of this file.

00:03:57.150 --> 00:04:00.040
It starts with metadata
written in YAML,

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.740
which is a list of
keys on the left

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.370
and their values on the right.

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:08.180
And the first key you see is
title, which is, in this case,

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:12.020
Lab 02 MoMA Museum Tour.

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:15.980
And the last section is
all about output in YAML.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.029
So here, the output
is an HTML document.

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:23.000
And beneath that, we can add
options to our HTML document,

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:29.340
for example, a table of contents
with the key toc set to true.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.370
So that's the metadata.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:33.530
But the real meat in
your R Markdown file

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:39.380
is text written in Markdown, and
of course, code written in R.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.380
So these three things,
metadata, text, and code,

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.870
make up an R Markdown document.

00:04:48.558 --> 00:04:50.460
ALISON HILL: So of
course, Desiree,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:54.120
the real magic happens when
you click on the Knit button

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:59.610
and see your file
turned into HTML.

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:01.320
So when we click
on the Knit button,

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.980
we can watch the build
happening in the bottom pane.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.620
Our R Markdown document
gets processed,

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:11.850
and you can see the
rendered HTML file locally.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:15.300
You can also choose to open
it up in a bigger browser

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.970
and explore around
by clicking on it,

00:05:17.970 --> 00:05:19.080
testing a few things out.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:28.570
DESIREE DE LEON: OK, so here's
a screenshot of what we just

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.520
showed you, what we knit.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:33.820
And we can see that using
the theme key in our YAML,

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:38.093
we were able to apply changes
to our fonts and colors.

00:05:38.093 --> 00:05:40.010
And we have this nice
table of contents that's

00:05:40.010 --> 00:05:41.052
floating off to the side.

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:46.430
This is still local and
stuck on my computer.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:50.260
A link would make it a lot
easier to share with learners.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:54.030
So how do we start sharing?

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:57.690
First, we need a web
server, which is basically

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.070
a big computer in the clouds.

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:03.380
And a hat tip to Heather
Nolis for that analogy.

00:06:03.380 --> 00:06:04.890
And for this
webinar, we're going

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:09.110
to use a web server
provided by Netlify.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:12.290
So we're going to drag and
drop that project folder

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:16.070
into Netlify, and this is going
to put our web page on line.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:19.220
So Alison, let's go ahead and
do our first drag and drop.

00:06:22.106 --> 00:06:24.900
ALISON HILL: Happy to, Desiree.

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:35.640
So I open up my browser,
go to app.netlify.com/drop.

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:36.900
And I type very slow.

00:06:40.730 --> 00:06:44.900
And then I'm going to see
that little box where it says,

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.180
drag and drop your
site folder here.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:49.250
I'm going to take
that Lab2 folder

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.930
and drag it right into that box.

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.160
You can see Netlify
thinks for a second,

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.650
and now my site is live.

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:00.760
And it gives me a nice
random link, friendly-borg,

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:04.180
that I can now share
with whomever I choose.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:04.870
Hurray!

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:05.230
DESIREE DE LEON: All right.

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:05.730
Hurray.

00:07:05.730 --> 00:07:09.590
We have deployed to Netlify.

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:12.410
But just a quick summary
of what happened.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:16.490
So we started with an R Markdown
file, and it was named index.

00:07:16.490 --> 00:07:22.790
We knit this to convert it to
a internet-ready HTML file.

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:24.980
That looked great, but it
still only lived locally

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:26.970
in our project folder.

00:07:26.970 --> 00:07:30.770
So we had to drag and drop it
into Netlify, which deployed

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.180
our page and generated a link.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:34.930
Woohoo.

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:39.100
OK, but this is just
one R Markdown file,

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:43.930
and we want to be able to
share many R Markdown files.

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:48.640
So we're going to hike
Markdown Mountain today.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.010
And our goal for the
rest of this webinar

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:53.710
is to show you four
R Markdown tools that

00:07:53.710 --> 00:07:59.680
are for turning a collection
of R Markdowns into a website.

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:03.100
And we're going to start with
the classic R Markdown site.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:06.250
So to make one, you just
need the R Markdown package

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:10.900
installed, which is already
done for you if you use RStudio.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:13.720
And here is an R Markdown
site for a course

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:15.160
about data visualization.

00:08:15.160 --> 00:08:16.340
This is just a screenshot.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:18.220
You can go to the link above it.

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:22.060
And actually, the MoMA lab that
we just turned into a web page

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:24.580
was one of the labs
from this course.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.440
Now, this course
web site is a place

00:08:26.440 --> 00:08:28.902
to share all of those labs.

00:08:28.902 --> 00:08:30.610
And the biggest addition
that we see here

00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:36.140
is a top navigation
bar, which allows

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.770
us to link to other web pages
including ones made with R

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:43.580
Markdown, like this page
with slides and readings,

00:08:43.580 --> 00:08:45.583
or other websites
like this one here,

00:08:45.583 --> 00:08:47.375
which goes to a learning
management system.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:50.740
All right so.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.220
Let's build a minimal R
Markdown site of our own.

00:08:54.220 --> 00:08:55.810
Imagine that this
box on the left

00:08:55.810 --> 00:09:00.700
is a list of files that are
inside of our project folder.

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:04.150
And to knit all of these pages
into our R Markdown site,

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:07.300
you can mouse over to
the Build tab in RStudio

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:10.580
and then click Build Website.

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:13.190
And after we've done that,
we see that it's added a new

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:17.770
folder called _site, and that's
where all of our HTML files

00:09:17.770 --> 00:09:18.270
live.

00:09:21.010 --> 00:09:24.490
And then we can also see the
site we just built locally.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:27.820
We have a nav bar, a
theme, and in this case,

00:09:27.820 --> 00:09:29.590
even a drop-down menu.

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:33.940
So let's look under the
hood at some of these files.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:36.970
And we'll start with index.Rmd.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:40.000
Index is a special file
name in any website,

00:09:40.000 --> 00:09:42.890
because it always
becomes the home page.

00:09:42.890 --> 00:09:45.730
So if each of our R
Markdown files in our site

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:47.560
was a hot-air
balloon here, index

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.890
would be the first one you see.

00:09:50.890 --> 00:09:53.390
And if we take a peek
inside index.Rmd,

00:09:53.390 --> 00:09:56.240
we actually don't see
anything very special.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:59.210
In fact, you can actually
take any existing R Markdown

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:02.510
document that you already
have, rename it index,

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:04.627
and turn it into
your site's homepage.

00:10:07.250 --> 00:10:10.400
The next file is _site.yml.

00:10:10.400 --> 00:10:11.420
And this is new.

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:14.870
So this file is in charge
of tying all of the pages

00:10:14.870 --> 00:10:15.770
together.

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:19.088
And it's a site configuration
file written in YAML.

00:10:19.088 --> 00:10:20.630
And let's take a
look inside of that.

00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:24.760
So the first thing
you may notice

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:29.440
is that this is a YAML file,
so there are no fences.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:31.810
It's just a list
of keys and values.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:34.810
And the first key
that we see is navbar.

00:10:34.810 --> 00:10:40.310
And this is what lets you
build your site's navigation.

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.620
There's a key for
title in this site

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:46.920
YAML, and whether we want
our links to be on the left,

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:49.550
but you could also
put them on the right.

00:10:49.550 --> 00:10:51.820
And if we want links
listed in a drop-down menu

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:55.670
we can do that too
from this file.

00:10:55.670 --> 00:10:58.410
And for all the pages you
link to in your app bar,

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:03.180
there is a key called href that
needs to point to an HTML file,

00:11:03.180 --> 00:11:06.980
even though you're using R
Markdown files as your source.

00:11:06.980 --> 00:11:10.230
You need to make sure that it's
always going to an HTML file.

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:14.780
And finally, we can set up a
theme that will apply to all

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:17.608
of the pages
throughout our site .

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:25.500
And as for other
R Markdown pages

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:28.300
there is nothing special
about them, either.

00:11:28.300 --> 00:11:31.420
As long as you link to a
page in your _site.yml,

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:34.180
you can take any existing
R Markdown file and turn it

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:37.840
into a page in your site.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:40.050
So a quiz, Alison.

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:44.310
Here is a page that's a
lab page on our website.

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:48.015
What do you think the YAML
for this lab page looks like?

00:11:48.015 --> 00:11:49.620
ALISON HILL: Oh, good question.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:56.100
I think that this YAML has
table of contents, toc, as one

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.230
of the keys that's set to true.

00:11:58.230 --> 00:12:00.000
And I see it's floating
off to the left,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.930
so I think toc_float
is also set to true.

00:12:03.930 --> 00:12:07.950
And I see my favorite
code, download key.

00:12:07.950 --> 00:12:10.650
So code_download, I
believe, is true also,

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:13.830
which allows you to click on
that nice little Download Rmd

00:12:13.830 --> 00:12:18.680
button, and somebody can
download your source Rmd file.

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:20.700
DESIREE DE LEON: OK,
Alison, good job.

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.860
That's exactly what we see.

00:12:22.860 --> 00:12:26.280
You can set the YAML for
each page if you want.

00:12:26.280 --> 00:12:30.150
It doesn't only have to be
done in the _site.yml file.

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:33.450
And if you want the output
options to apply to every

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:35.820
single page, that's when
you would choose to put it

00:12:35.820 --> 00:12:40.850
in the _site.yml file, as
opposed to the individual page.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:45.080
But OK, so finally we are ready
to share our R Markdown sites.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.350
And we'll do this the
same way as we did before.

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:52.070
As a reminder, all of our HTML
website-ready pages are stored

00:12:52.070 --> 00:12:54.330
in this _site folder.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:58.410
So this is the only folder
that we need to drag and drop.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:00.800
So Alison, can you
take it away again?

00:13:00.800 --> 00:13:02.990
ALISON HILL: I will.

00:13:02.990 --> 00:13:06.800
So here we're back at
app.netlify.com/drop,

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:09.590
and we're dropping that
_site folder right in.

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:15.106
And we're able to get a
link, hardcore-goldwasser.

00:13:17.647 --> 00:13:18.480
Great name, website.

00:13:22.192 --> 00:13:25.320
DESIREE DE LEON: OK, thank you.

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.950
So one thing you may have
noticed with what Alison just

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:31.890
showed you is that there is a
note from Netlify, and it says,

00:13:31.890 --> 00:13:34.960
unclaimed sites are
deleted after 24 hours.

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:37.620
So we have to actually create
an account with Netlify

00:13:37.620 --> 00:13:39.330
to keep our site
online permanently.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:41.970
So let's actually go back
to Netlify and do that.

00:13:46.398 --> 00:13:48.190
ALISON HILL: So we
click on the little Sign

00:13:48.190 --> 00:13:50.620
Up for Netlify button,
and you can sign up

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:52.390
with an existing
version control account,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.560
like GitHub, GitLab,
or BitBucket.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:56.080
So if you have one,
I encourage you

00:13:56.080 --> 00:13:57.927
to use those to
sign in to Netlify,

00:13:57.927 --> 00:13:59.510
because that'll make
your life easier.

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:05.403
And today, we're going
to actually sign up

00:14:05.403 --> 00:14:06.320
with an email address.

00:14:06.320 --> 00:14:09.440
So Desiree is entering an
email address, signing up

00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:11.075
for an account with Netlify.

00:14:15.115 --> 00:14:17.615
She's waiting on instructions
for how to verify her account.

00:14:27.140 --> 00:14:32.890
And we have it, and we
will be able to own,

00:14:32.890 --> 00:14:34.870
was it hardcore-goldwasser
forever?

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:37.205
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:44.710
So now it's part of her
team on her Netlify account,

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:50.025
and your silly-named
link is now here to stay.

00:14:50.025 --> 00:14:51.150
DESIREE DE LEON: All right.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:51.650
Great.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:59.280
OK, but after I've deployed my
site, what happens if I decide

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:02.870
that I want to make a change?

00:15:02.870 --> 00:15:05.290
So let's say, for
example, looking

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:09.070
at the YAML file
on the left side,

00:15:09.070 --> 00:15:11.860
say that I want to change
my theme to something

00:15:11.860 --> 00:15:13.210
named United.

00:15:13.210 --> 00:15:14.830
So once I make
that change, I need

00:15:14.830 --> 00:15:16.460
to remember to
rebuild my website.

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:24.750
And then I need to be able
to drag and drop again.

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:26.670
So let's see what that
process looks like.

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:35.760
ALISON HILL: Sorry, my
mouse has gone missing.

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:36.968
DESIREE DE LEON: There we go.

00:15:39.370 --> 00:15:43.200
OK, so once we've rebuilt our
site with the changes that we

00:15:43.200 --> 00:15:47.740
want, we can just use
that _site folder again,

00:15:47.740 --> 00:15:51.692
but we have to go into
Deploys in our Netlify UI,

00:15:51.692 --> 00:15:54.150
and then you see the little
box, "Need to update your site?

00:15:54.150 --> 00:15:55.865
Drag and drop your
site folder here.

00:15:55.865 --> 00:15:57.240
So this means that
we get to keep

00:15:57.240 --> 00:16:00.240
that link, the
hardcore-goldwasser link,

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.300
and update the same link
with our new content.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:07.860
So if we drag and drop it again
it takes a second to think,

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:09.480
and it republishes.

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:10.980
So now if we click
on the link, we

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:13.842
can see that our new theme, the
United theme, has been applied.

00:16:13.842 --> 00:16:15.300
So it looks a little
bit different,

00:16:15.300 --> 00:16:18.260
but the link is the same.

00:16:18.260 --> 00:16:18.760
Great.

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.970
So now you know how to build
and update R Markdown sites.

00:16:24.970 --> 00:16:26.830
And before we move
on to the next tool,

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:29.800
I just want to highlight
some good and bad points

00:16:29.800 --> 00:16:32.500
to take away about using
this tool for a course.

00:16:32.500 --> 00:16:35.800
So some really great things is
that the source stays the same.

00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:38.350
You can use a lot of
your existing documents

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:40.390
and just plop them
right in without having

00:16:40.390 --> 00:16:41.775
to change much at all.

00:16:41.775 --> 00:16:43.150
And another really
great thing is

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:44.608
that you have these
built-in themes

00:16:44.608 --> 00:16:47.470
you can take advantage of,
to give your site a makeover

00:16:47.470 --> 00:16:48.980
easily.

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:51.200
A bad thing about
R Markdown sites

00:16:51.200 --> 00:16:55.190
is that as you start to
add many different pages,

00:16:55.190 --> 00:16:58.130
your directory can get really,
really full, because R Markdown

00:16:58.130 --> 00:17:01.010
sites do not allow you to
organize pages and R Markdown

00:17:01.010 --> 00:17:02.940
documents into sub-folders.

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:05.480
So that's just one
thing to keep in mind.

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:07.369
But with that, let's
go ahead and move on

00:17:07.369 --> 00:17:11.510
to our next tool,
which is Distill.

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:15.567
So our next stop on Markdown
Mountain is Distill websites.

00:17:15.567 --> 00:17:17.150
And you'll have to
install the Distill

00:17:17.150 --> 00:17:18.520
package before you can use it.

00:17:21.300 --> 00:17:24.960
And I just want to give you
a quick tour of the Distill

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.560
websites.

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:32.050
So let's go ahead
and press Play here.

00:17:32.050 --> 00:17:35.080
And the style of a Distill
site is actually not

00:17:35.080 --> 00:17:38.800
very easy to change without CSS,
but it looks pretty slick out

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:39.430
of the box.

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:42.130
So you can think of Distill
as being a little bit

00:17:42.130 --> 00:17:45.430
like the nerdier sibling
of an R Markdown site.

00:17:45.430 --> 00:17:48.010
It's built for much more
technical and scientific

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:49.290
communication.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:00.650
So for example, there is a
way to list multiple authors

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.230
and their affiliations.

00:18:03.230 --> 00:18:08.130
You can also include footers and
asides in your Distill pages.

00:18:08.130 --> 00:18:11.550
And there's this really nice
way to display reuse licenses

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:12.660
for educational content.

00:18:15.350 --> 00:18:17.600
So this is some of the
features in the Distill website

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:18.780
that come out of the box.

00:18:18.780 --> 00:18:21.920
And so now let's build a
minimal Distill site of our own.

00:18:21.920 --> 00:18:24.110
And just like we did
with the R Markdown site,

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:27.650
we're going to use the Build
tab to knit some files that

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:29.630
are in our project directory.

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:32.660
And once we do that, it's
going to create a folder,

00:18:32.660 --> 00:18:38.470
again called _site, that's
going to house our HTML files.

00:18:38.470 --> 00:18:43.670
Here is what our minimal Distill
site's going to look like.

00:18:43.670 --> 00:18:47.990
And just to point out,
we can add a description.

00:18:47.990 --> 00:18:50.510
We can have those
multiple authors.

00:18:50.510 --> 00:18:52.820
We can still retain that
drop-down menu that we also

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:56.160
had in R Markdown sites.

00:18:56.160 --> 00:18:58.410
So let's look at some of
these files a little bit more

00:18:58.410 --> 00:19:01.290
closely, starting
with the index page.

00:19:01.290 --> 00:19:04.530
Again, this is going to be our
home page in a Distill website,

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:08.887
and any existing R Markdown
document that you have will do.

00:19:08.887 --> 00:19:11.220
There's just one addition we
need to make inside of this

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:17.270
file, and that is that in
the YAML of the index file,

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:20.460
we need to be able to
add this line, site:

00:19:20.460 --> 00:19:23.300
distill::distill_website.

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:25.950
And it's also nice to include a
title and a short description,

00:19:25.950 --> 00:19:27.320
but not necessary.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:33.770
And then we also have
an _site.yml file.

00:19:33.770 --> 00:19:36.110
This is still going to be
our site configuration,

00:19:36.110 --> 00:19:37.880
so this should also
sound a bit familiar.

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:44.070
And the YAML navbar
keys here are

00:19:44.070 --> 00:19:46.260
similar to what we used
in R Markdown sites,

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.510
with one important change.

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:51.510
The output must
specify that this is

00:19:51.510 --> 00:19:53.430
going to be a distill_article.

00:19:57.100 --> 00:19:59.740
And there is one more
thing I need to tell you

00:19:59.740 --> 00:20:02.790
about Distill websites.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:04.500
You can make part of
your Distill website

00:20:04.500 --> 00:20:08.780
into a blog, which
looks like this.

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:11.230
So essentially, you can
have a single page made up

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:12.760
of clickable posts.

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:14.950
And each post itself
is its own page

00:20:14.950 --> 00:20:17.290
and it doesn't need to be
built into the navigation

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:22.910
bar the way that other parts
of R Markdown sites need to be

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:25.050
or like Distill
sites need to be.

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.640
And this can be useful in a
classroom setting just if you

00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:30.820
want to post different
things like announcements

00:20:30.820 --> 00:20:34.650
in a running list sort of way.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:37.860
So in order to create
a blog-like page,

00:20:37.860 --> 00:20:40.950
we first need to create
what's called a listing page.

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:43.710
And this is an R
Markdown document

00:20:43.710 --> 00:20:46.620
whose only job is to
have a title and then

00:20:46.620 --> 00:20:49.080
a listing, the YAML
key, and value.

00:20:49.080 --> 00:20:51.270
And we're going to call
our listing page Schedule.

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:57.370
And then we have to remember
to link this listing page

00:20:57.370 --> 00:21:01.550
to the navigation
bar within _site.yml.

00:21:01.550 --> 00:21:03.520
And once we've done
that, then we're

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:05.620
ready to make some blog posts.

00:21:08.300 --> 00:21:11.170
So the most straightforward
way to create a post is to run

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:14.170
the code distill::create_post.

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:17.690
And then within the parentheses,
you put the title of your post.

00:21:17.690 --> 00:21:20.620
And this is automatically going
to create a new folder called

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:24.190
_posts with a subdirectory
for your post.

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:26.710
And it will also
trigger a little window

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:30.430
to open with your R Markdown
file, ready for you to edit.

00:21:30.430 --> 00:21:32.350
And you can rinse and
repeat this process,

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:34.100
creating as many new
posts as you'd like.

00:21:34.100 --> 00:21:37.428
So let's go ahead and just look
inside one of those post files.

00:21:37.428 --> 00:21:39.970
On the left here, you can see
what I mean when I said that it

00:21:39.970 --> 00:21:42.490
creates a little subdirectory
for your post underneath

00:21:42.490 --> 00:21:43.872
the _posts folder.

00:21:43.872 --> 00:21:45.330
And all of this is
created for you,

00:21:45.330 --> 00:21:46.510
so you don't really
have to worry

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:48.410
too much about remembering
the order here.

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:51.980
But if we look inside
of the post itself,

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:55.780
we see that the YAML is already
automatically populated for us

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:57.920
with the information
that we need.

00:21:57.920 --> 00:22:02.275
We can edit it if we need to
change the date or the title.

00:22:02.275 --> 00:22:04.150
And the only downside
here is that once we're

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:06.130
done creating our post,
we have to remember

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:09.100
to knit this post individually.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.050
If you don't knit it
on its own, then it

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:14.950
won't show up on
your listing page.

00:22:14.950 --> 00:22:16.650
So once we've created
all of our posts,

00:22:16.650 --> 00:22:20.760
we can then remember to build
our website one last time

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:23.943
so that the listing page will
be updated with any posts

00:22:23.943 --> 00:22:24.735
that we've created.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.490
And here, for example, is what
our very minimal blog listing

00:22:31.490 --> 00:22:33.210
page looks like.

00:22:33.210 --> 00:22:35.810
So on the left, you see
what the listing page is,

00:22:35.810 --> 00:22:39.020
and then you can click on to
any of those individual links

00:22:39.020 --> 00:22:40.460
to see what the post looks like.

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:45.870
And just as before, once
we're ready to share,

00:22:45.870 --> 00:22:48.530
we can use our old
drag-and-drop method.

00:22:48.530 --> 00:22:54.323
So we'll take the _site folder
and drag that in to Netlify,

00:22:54.323 --> 00:22:55.865
just as we did with
R Markdown sites.

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:02.680
So with that, we've covered
two R Markdown tools.

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:04.150
And there's just
a couple of points

00:23:04.150 --> 00:23:05.942
I want to highlight
about Distill before we

00:23:05.942 --> 00:23:08.230
move on to the next tool.

00:23:08.230 --> 00:23:10.510
Really nice things
about Distill are

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:13.990
that you have these really great
design features out of the box.

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:15.310
You have two content flavors.

00:23:15.310 --> 00:23:17.150
You can have a regular page.

00:23:17.150 --> 00:23:19.300
But you can also have
this blog-like page,

00:23:19.300 --> 00:23:24.010
which just lets you show some
more chronological content.

00:23:24.010 --> 00:23:25.360
One bad thing about Distill--

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:28.450
it's just pretty
annoying, I guess,

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:32.980
to have to remember to knit
each individual post one by one

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:35.632
if you would like to take
advantage of the blog feature.

00:23:35.632 --> 00:23:37.090
But the good thing
there, at least,

00:23:37.090 --> 00:23:40.570
is that you don't have to do
anything manual to make sure

00:23:40.570 --> 00:23:43.045
that your posts get on that
listing page we created.

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:49.660
OK, so now for the
rest of the tools,

00:23:49.660 --> 00:23:53.560
Alison will be our guide for
our ascent up Markdown Mountain.

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:57.070
And we have two
more tools to go.

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.470
ALISON HILL: Thank
you, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:02.770
So I'm going to
describe bookdown now,

00:24:02.770 --> 00:24:06.070
which is another R package
that you need to install

00:24:06.070 --> 00:24:09.040
before being able to use it.

00:24:09.040 --> 00:24:11.500
And as the package
name suggests,

00:24:11.500 --> 00:24:14.830
bookdown is a way to build
books with R markdown.

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:18.130
And you can use it to build
an online book with a table

00:24:18.130 --> 00:24:23.560
of contents, chapters-- in fact,
as many chapters as you want--

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:25.450
and lots of bells
and whistles that we

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:28.050
won't be able to cover today.

00:24:28.050 --> 00:24:30.140
But they're all actually
covered in the bookdown

00:24:30.140 --> 00:24:32.210
book, which you can see here.

00:24:32.210 --> 00:24:35.640
And it also happens to be
a bookdown book itself.

00:24:35.640 --> 00:24:40.472
So you can see that
we have a website here

00:24:40.472 --> 00:24:42.680
that looks a little bit
different than the R Markdown

00:24:42.680 --> 00:24:45.500
and Distill sites that
Desiree just showed us.

00:24:45.500 --> 00:24:47.420
Instead of having
that top navigation

00:24:47.420 --> 00:24:49.910
bar that allowed us
to navigate and find

00:24:49.910 --> 00:24:51.740
the different pages
in our site, now we

00:24:51.740 --> 00:24:56.553
have this table of contents
over here in this left sidebar.

00:24:56.553 --> 00:24:58.470
So when I click on that
little hamburger menu,

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:02.000
I can see my table of
contents going up and down,

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:03.020
and I can scroll it.

00:25:03.020 --> 00:25:04.780
It's a very long
table of contents,

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:07.460
but you can see that I have
a lot of materials in here,

00:25:07.460 --> 00:25:09.362
and I can click around
to any of these.

00:25:09.362 --> 00:25:10.820
And you can see
that the right pane

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:14.820
shows me the content of
that page as I click around.

00:25:14.820 --> 00:25:18.200
So here, in a bookdown
book, each of these chapters

00:25:18.200 --> 00:25:20.190
is actually an R Markdown file.

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:22.610
And when I click on it, I'm
able to see the content,

00:25:22.610 --> 00:25:26.060
but I can still access the
entire table of contents here.

00:25:26.060 --> 00:25:28.720
And you can imagine that if you
have this many documents in a R

00:25:28.720 --> 00:25:30.178
Markdown or Distill
site, you would

00:25:30.178 --> 00:25:32.180
have had to build
them, usually manually,

00:25:32.180 --> 00:25:34.025
into some kind of
top navigation bar.

00:25:34.025 --> 00:25:35.900
And you just don't have
that much real estate

00:25:35.900 --> 00:25:38.885
going across the top as you
do with this kind of layout.

00:25:38.885 --> 00:25:40.760
So that's one of the
reasons that bookdown is

00:25:40.760 --> 00:25:43.200
really popular for educators.

00:25:43.200 --> 00:25:46.280
bookdown also comes
with search built in,

00:25:46.280 --> 00:25:48.590
so you're able to
search the entire book.

00:25:48.590 --> 00:25:50.090
So this is also
another nice feature

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:52.160
that allows you to
leverage your content.

00:25:57.130 --> 00:25:59.800
So here's an example of a
much shorter bookdown book

00:25:59.800 --> 00:26:02.750
that I built to show
you for a demonstration.

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.480
So we can give you a quick tour
of the files that go into it.

00:26:05.480 --> 00:26:08.380
So this book has three
labs from a course

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:10.300
I taught on data visualization.

00:26:10.300 --> 00:26:13.420
The first one is about Nathan's
Hot Dog Eating Contests.

00:26:13.420 --> 00:26:16.330
The second one is the one
that Desiree just showed you,

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:18.050
the MoMA museum tour.

00:26:18.050 --> 00:26:20.230
And the third one
was a lesson teaching

00:26:20.230 --> 00:26:22.570
about colors with
ggplot, using data

00:26:22.570 --> 00:26:27.330
about children's production
of animal sounds.

00:26:27.330 --> 00:26:30.510
So the files in this book
project here are on the left.

00:26:30.510 --> 00:26:33.690
So you can see that I have
a few R Markdown files.

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:35.370
I have a YAML file.

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:36.910
It's an R project.

00:26:36.910 --> 00:26:37.920
I have some folders.

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:41.390
And I have a very important
folder called _book.

00:26:41.390 --> 00:26:43.500
Now, Desiree, do you
happen to remember

00:26:43.500 --> 00:26:46.835
what the output folder was for
R Markdown and Distill sites?

00:26:46.835 --> 00:26:47.960
DESIREE DE LEON: Yes, I do.

00:26:47.960 --> 00:26:50.295
It was _site.

00:26:50.295 --> 00:26:51.180
ALISON HILL: Yes!

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:56.740
So with a bookdown book,
now our book is in _book.

00:26:56.740 --> 00:26:59.910
So when I have all of these
files that you see over here

00:26:59.910 --> 00:27:03.180
in the left pane, when I
have all those in a directory

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:07.170
for an R project, and I
mouse over to my Build tab,

00:27:07.170 --> 00:27:10.260
and I click on that
Build book icon,

00:27:10.260 --> 00:27:12.450
it creates a new
folder called _book.

00:27:12.450 --> 00:27:15.000
And that's where my
book now lives locally.

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:18.500
So I'm going to show you
inside some of those files.

00:27:18.500 --> 00:27:21.610
So let's take a look
first at index.Rmd.

00:27:21.610 --> 00:27:24.430
So Desiree described
this as the home page

00:27:24.430 --> 00:27:26.650
for your other R Markdown
sites, and it's actually

00:27:26.650 --> 00:27:28.600
the home page,
also, for bookdown.

00:27:28.600 --> 00:27:32.680
It also happens to turn
into your first chapter.

00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:35.770
So if you take a look inside,
it looks pretty familiar.

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:37.210
There is YAML at the top.

00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:40.540
We have a title, a
subtitle, an author.

00:27:40.540 --> 00:27:43.100
We also have two new
YAML keys on the bottom.

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:45.700
And these are critical for
building a bookdown book.

00:27:45.700 --> 00:27:47.680
They are site and output.

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:50.330
So both of those are
necessary for building and H2

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:53.450
in my book like the
one we're building now.

00:27:53.450 --> 00:27:56.450
And then the rest of this file,
everything underneath the YAML,

00:27:56.450 --> 00:27:58.250
can be a regular
R Markdown file.

00:27:58.250 --> 00:28:02.570
It just also happens
to turn into chapter 1.

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:04.880
So like with our
other sites, we also

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:06.710
have a separate
[INAUDIBLE] file, too.

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:11.090
So in the [INAUDIBLE] R Markdown
and Distill sites that Desiree

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:15.380
demonstrated, you
had a _site.yml file.

00:28:15.380 --> 00:28:19.100
And in bookdown, it's
an _bookdown.yml file.

00:28:19.100 --> 00:28:22.940
And this file works in concert
with the index.Rmd file

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:27.960
to guide how all of our
chapters [INAUDIBLE] together.

00:28:27.960 --> 00:28:31.970
So I'm going to recommend that
your _bookdown.yml file is

00:28:31.970 --> 00:28:33.650
pretty sparse.

00:28:33.650 --> 00:28:36.890
The main key that I recommend
using for this YAML file

00:28:36.890 --> 00:28:38.060
is new_session.

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:40.230
And I recommend that
you set it to true.

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:43.070
This means that each of
your R Markdown chapters

00:28:43.070 --> 00:28:45.638
is knit in a separate section.

00:28:45.638 --> 00:28:47.930
This will make your life
easier and will make it easier

00:28:47.930 --> 00:28:50.690
for you to troubleshoot
when you're knitting

00:28:50.690 --> 00:28:52.580
your book, building your book.

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:54.890
Any of the errors
that you see will

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:57.230
be a lot easier to debug
if each of the chapters

00:28:57.230 --> 00:28:59.960
is totally independent.

00:28:59.960 --> 00:29:02.000
You might notice that
my chapter files here

00:29:02.000 --> 00:29:05.370
on the left are now named
with numbers as prefixes.

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:10.460
So I have 01-hotdogs,
02-moma, and 03-meow.

00:29:10.460 --> 00:29:15.290
You can use the _bookdown.yml
file to instead list the order

00:29:15.290 --> 00:29:17.450
of your R Markdown chapters.

00:29:17.450 --> 00:29:20.210
So before, it was using the
number of chapters to determine

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:22.880
the order that the
book got built.

00:29:22.880 --> 00:29:26.300
You can also take away
those numbers from the names

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:29.780
of the files and use the
_bookdown.yml to determine

00:29:29.780 --> 00:29:32.480
the order of the chapters as
they are presented to you.

00:29:32.480 --> 00:29:35.690
So that's another way that
you can use this _bookdown.yml

00:29:35.690 --> 00:29:38.490
file.

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:40.810
And the chapters themselves,
I have three of them

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:44.080
here in addition
to the index.Rmd.

00:29:44.080 --> 00:29:46.160
Those are each separate
R Markdown files,

00:29:46.160 --> 00:29:49.030
but there was one major change.

00:29:49.030 --> 00:29:51.190
And that is that each
of these chapters

00:29:51.190 --> 00:29:54.190
has to begin with a
level 1 Markdown header.

00:29:54.190 --> 00:29:57.610
So here you see hashtag
MoMA Museum Tour.

00:29:57.610 --> 00:30:00.490
Each of these chapters
has to start with that.

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:03.040
And so that means that none
of your chapters in a bookdown

00:30:03.040 --> 00:30:05.840
book can have its own YAML.

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:08.540
And so this is probably
the biggest problem

00:30:08.540 --> 00:30:10.540
if you're going to be
trying to convert existing

00:30:10.540 --> 00:30:11.920
material into bookdown.

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:13.450
It means that
you're going to have

00:30:13.450 --> 00:30:16.840
to remove the YAML from each
individual R Markdown file that

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:18.040
will go into a chapter.

00:30:18.040 --> 00:30:21.820
So each chapter has to start
with a level 1 Markdown header

00:30:21.820 --> 00:30:24.160
and can't have its own YAML.

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:26.852
They actually [INAUDIBLE]
the YAML from the index.Rmd.

00:30:29.183 --> 00:30:30.600
And so this gives
you a little bit

00:30:30.600 --> 00:30:33.690
of an overview of what
bookdown is doing.

00:30:33.690 --> 00:30:37.560
So what you make is, you make
these separate R Markdown

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:39.130
files for each chapter.

00:30:39.130 --> 00:30:42.270
So here we have index.Rmd,
And then another Rmd,

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:43.380
and another Rmd.

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:46.930
And each of those begins with
that level 1 chapter heading.

00:30:46.930 --> 00:30:49.500
So those are all your
separate R Markdown files.

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:51.630
And remember that index.Rmd
is the special one that

00:30:51.630 --> 00:30:53.246
also includes a YAML.

00:30:53.246 --> 00:30:57.130
[INAUDIBLE] bookdown does
when you have this new_session

00:30:57.130 --> 00:30:57.630
true--

00:30:57.630 --> 00:30:58.740
[AUDIO OUT]

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:02.070
--in the bookdown YAML is that
it knits of these R Markdown

00:31:02.070 --> 00:31:02.580
files in--

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:05.160
[AUDIO OUT]

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:06.140
--page.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:08.090
So each of those
is now independent.

00:31:08.090 --> 00:31:09.680
But how do we get
to the point where

00:31:09.680 --> 00:31:11.220
we're seeing them all together?

00:31:11.220 --> 00:31:14.480
book down merges
them and gives us

00:31:14.480 --> 00:31:17.840
the nice, interactive
online navigable

00:31:17.840 --> 00:31:20.510
book that we see when
we build the book.

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:25.430
So how do we share the
book once it's actually

00:31:25.430 --> 00:31:26.780
written and built?

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:30.480
We know our HTML files
live in that _book folder.

00:31:30.480 --> 00:31:33.710
So this should seem
like old hat to you now.

00:31:33.710 --> 00:31:38.930
What I would do is go
to app.netlify.com/drop,

00:31:38.930 --> 00:31:43.340
and I [INAUDIBLE] I can drop
that _book folder into the same

00:31:43.340 --> 00:31:44.970
spot.

00:31:44.970 --> 00:31:46.272
And that would give me a link.

00:31:46.272 --> 00:31:48.730
But you might have noticed that
some of those random domain

00:31:48.730 --> 00:31:50.440
names are hard to remember
and keep track of.

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:51.857
I believe the one
that Desiree was

00:31:51.857 --> 00:31:55.780
working with was called
hardcore-goldwasser.

00:31:55.780 --> 00:31:57.580
It's good for flying
under the radar,

00:31:57.580 --> 00:32:00.220
but they're probably
not great for learners,

00:32:00.220 --> 00:32:02.680
and they're probably not
easy for you to share.

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:04.540
So we can update the
domain on netlify.com

00:32:04.540 --> 00:32:08.180
to make the link easier to
share with other people.

00:32:08.180 --> 00:32:12.430
So in this example, our random
domain was stoic-babbage.

00:32:12.430 --> 00:32:15.310
So if you go into the
Deploys tab on Netlify

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:17.230
once you've made
your account, you

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:19.240
can see that that's
my default subdomain.

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:22.810
And if I click on
Options, Edit Site Name,

00:32:22.810 --> 00:32:23.770
then I can change it.

00:32:23.770 --> 00:32:28.150
So here I'm going to
change it to dataviz-book,

00:32:28.150 --> 00:32:30.160
which amazingly was not taken.

00:32:30.160 --> 00:32:33.175
So now I have the domain
dataviz-book.netlify.com.

00:32:35.956 --> 00:32:38.060
And you can see
it takes a second,

00:32:38.060 --> 00:32:39.430
but it is already deployed.

00:32:39.430 --> 00:32:41.530
The new link works right away.

00:32:41.530 --> 00:32:43.900
And now, because I have
an account with Netlify

00:32:43.900 --> 00:32:46.660
and I've changed that domain,
that's my domain forever.

00:32:46.660 --> 00:32:48.260
I can keep that.

00:32:48.260 --> 00:32:49.880
And I can always
update that as well,

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:51.130
the way we showed you earlier.

00:32:54.510 --> 00:32:56.270
So the best part
about bookdown is

00:32:56.270 --> 00:32:58.730
that I think it's hard to
get lost in a bookdown book.

00:32:58.730 --> 00:33:00.973
It's [INAUDIBLE]
default. You think

00:33:00.973 --> 00:33:02.390
it's going to be
long, because you

00:33:02.390 --> 00:33:04.720
have this big table of
contents in the left side.

00:33:04.720 --> 00:33:06.380
So that works in your favor.

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:09.170
People are used to being able
to scroll up and down vertically

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:10.940
in that table of contents.

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:12.687
And the search
functions help as well.

00:33:12.687 --> 00:33:14.270
And so since you
have more real estate

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:15.830
in the table of
contents vertically,

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:17.930
you can also add a
lot more content then.

00:33:17.930 --> 00:33:20.390
So it may be easier when
you have longer-form content

00:33:20.390 --> 00:33:23.060
for teaching, as opposed
to an R Markdown site

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:25.580
or a Distill site, where you
have to manually build up

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:27.920
that site navigation.

00:33:27.920 --> 00:33:30.770
The hard parts of bookdown
are that if you're

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:34.550
converting existing R Markdown
files over to this new format,

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:36.080
you're going to
have to do some work

00:33:36.080 --> 00:33:40.910
to be able to remove those
YAMLs from your individual files

00:33:40.910 --> 00:33:44.600
and start them all with those
level 1 chapter headers.

00:33:44.600 --> 00:33:47.960
You may also need to
rename your files.

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:50.780
And you also still don't
get to have subfolders.

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:53.210
So you're still going to
have all your R Markdown

00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:54.530
files in one directory.

00:33:54.530 --> 00:33:56.570
And so once you get a
certain number of these,

00:33:56.570 --> 00:33:59.750
it can pretty quickly
feel fairly messy.

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:05.420
So, Desiree, we have made it to
the summit, which is blogdown.

00:34:05.420 --> 00:34:06.510
DESIREE DE LEON: We have!

00:34:06.510 --> 00:34:07.090
Yes.

00:34:07.090 --> 00:34:07.590
Hooray!

00:34:11.850 --> 00:34:13.800
ALISON HILL: So blogdown
is a great package

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:16.650
for building custom and unique
websites with R Markdown,

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:18.900
but it is not for
the faint of heart.

00:34:18.900 --> 00:34:21.179
It is also probably not
the best choice for you

00:34:21.179 --> 00:34:23.520
to adopt on complete
short notice,

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:25.195
because there is a lot to learn.

00:34:25.195 --> 00:34:27.570
But I wanted to make sure that
you know about it, in case

00:34:27.570 --> 00:34:29.862
you've already tried the
tools that we've just covered.

00:34:29.862 --> 00:34:32.639
So if you've already tried R
Markdown sites, Distill sites,

00:34:32.639 --> 00:34:35.190
or bookdown, and you're
still not quite satisfied

00:34:35.190 --> 00:34:38.550
with the options and you want
something highly customizable,

00:34:38.550 --> 00:34:40.920
then you may [INAUDIBLE]
[? look up ?] blogdown.

00:34:40.920 --> 00:34:44.280
Or for other folks who have a
little bit more time to prep,

00:34:44.280 --> 00:34:47.280
if you're looking at a
fall or winter quarter

00:34:47.280 --> 00:34:49.409
class for next year,
you could think

00:34:49.409 --> 00:34:52.530
about making a website
using blogdown.

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:54.690
So what is blogdown?

00:34:54.690 --> 00:34:57.330
It's an R package that helps
you build a website using

00:34:57.330 --> 00:34:59.700
a generator called Hugo.

00:34:59.700 --> 00:35:02.200
And the package itself
lets you use the RStudio

00:35:02.200 --> 00:35:07.020
IDE to build your site and to
use R Markdown content to make

00:35:07.020 --> 00:35:09.480
your site.

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:12.840
So one of the best and
worst things about Hugo

00:35:12.840 --> 00:35:17.760
is that you now have access
to over 100 Hugo themes

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:18.750
to choose from.

00:35:18.750 --> 00:35:22.020
So you can get really
creative with it.

00:35:22.020 --> 00:35:24.650
So you may have felt like when
you were looking at bookdown

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:27.360
sites or Distill or
R Markdown sites--

00:35:27.360 --> 00:35:28.950
you've probably
seen a lot of sites

00:35:28.950 --> 00:35:30.990
that look just like those.

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:32.407
And there's some
benefits to that.

00:35:32.407 --> 00:35:34.782
I don't think there's anything
wrong with using a default

00:35:34.782 --> 00:35:35.310
site.

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.770
But a lot of people want
to go a little bit further,

00:35:37.770 --> 00:35:40.050
and they have
specialized needs that

00:35:40.050 --> 00:35:42.960
require specialized solutions.

00:35:42.960 --> 00:35:45.030
But it's also easy to
get very overwhelmed

00:35:45.030 --> 00:35:46.740
by the choices of Hugo themes.

00:35:49.600 --> 00:35:53.440
So for my own course
websites and when I teach,

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:56.860
my favorite thing to use
is the Hugo Academic theme,

00:35:56.860 --> 00:35:58.930
because it is very
well documented,

00:35:58.930 --> 00:36:01.360
and it's very flexible, also.

00:36:01.360 --> 00:36:04.940
The theme also has built-in
search, which is really nice.

00:36:04.940 --> 00:36:08.380
So here is the documentation
theme for Academic.

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:10.060
It's a demonstration site.

00:36:10.060 --> 00:36:14.207
You can see that I have a little
search button up at the top.

00:36:14.207 --> 00:36:15.790
And you can see that
there's all kinds

00:36:15.790 --> 00:36:17.132
of different content in here.

00:36:17.132 --> 00:36:19.090
And it's important to
remember with Hugo sites,

00:36:19.090 --> 00:36:20.680
when you look at a
demonstration site,

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:22.138
it's trying to show
you everything.

00:36:22.138 --> 00:36:24.160
It's not necessarily
showing you the best way

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:26.650
to lay out everything.

00:36:26.650 --> 00:36:31.120
So I built a demonstration
site for this webinar

00:36:31.120 --> 00:36:35.350
using the Hugo Academic
theme and porting over

00:36:35.350 --> 00:36:38.650
my existing content
from the course I taught

00:36:38.650 --> 00:36:41.260
on data visualization,
where I had that R Markdown

00:36:41.260 --> 00:36:44.480
site that Desiree actually
showed at the very beginning.

00:36:44.480 --> 00:36:46.850
So I converted all of that
content over to blogdown

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:49.030
to show you what's possible.

00:36:49.030 --> 00:36:50.890
So here is my blogdown site.

00:36:50.890 --> 00:36:55.550
And you can access it at
share-blogdown.netlify.com.

00:36:55.550 --> 00:36:58.390
And you can see I have
my nice built-in search.

00:36:58.390 --> 00:37:00.532
I have this top nav bar.

00:37:00.532 --> 00:37:02.740
And you can see I have some
nice features, like being

00:37:02.740 --> 00:37:06.005
able to link to my
RStudio Cloud, perhaps,

00:37:06.005 --> 00:37:09.160
or my Piazza, GitHub.

00:37:09.160 --> 00:37:11.930
You can put all those
links in one place.

00:37:11.930 --> 00:37:14.200
You can also see that I
have some interesting ways

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:16.600
to lay out content that are
a little bit more varied

00:37:16.600 --> 00:37:20.220
than what we had at our
fingertips with R Markdown,

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:22.840
Distill, and bookdown sites.

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:27.640
Here, I've also used blog
posts to lay out a schedule.

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:32.290
And this was inspired by
[INAUDIBLE] course website.

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:36.180
So showing you each of the labs
stacked on top of each other.

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:37.150
Where you click on--

00:37:37.150 --> 00:37:37.820
[AUDIO OUT]

00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:41.710
--virtual lab, the
content for that lab.

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:45.520
You can also see a course
section for the syllabus,

00:37:45.520 --> 00:37:49.480
and finally some instructors
with pictures, links,

00:37:49.480 --> 00:37:50.840
all of the things.

00:37:50.840 --> 00:37:52.900
There's also a
Resources section here

00:37:52.900 --> 00:37:54.610
that shows a different
kind of layout,

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:58.690
where you can share different
materials with users.

00:37:58.690 --> 00:38:00.190
Just another different
option that's

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:03.590
available with the
Hugo Academic theme.

00:38:03.590 --> 00:38:06.620
So here, if you remember,
I showed you this schedule.

00:38:06.620 --> 00:38:09.680
And if I clicked on this Lab
02, the MoMA Museum Tour,

00:38:09.680 --> 00:38:13.880
that's actually the lab that
Desiree started with initially.

00:38:13.880 --> 00:38:16.190
So let me show you
what that looks like.

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:17.720
You would go to
that page, and you

00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:20.900
would see these two [INAUDIBLE]
here, Lab and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:38:20.900 --> 00:38:23.510
And what each of those
goes to is actually,

00:38:23.510 --> 00:38:25.790
the lab goes to an
R Markdown site.

00:38:25.790 --> 00:38:28.490
So this is housed
within my blogdown site,

00:38:28.490 --> 00:38:30.830
but I didn't have to change
anything about that file.

00:38:30.830 --> 00:38:33.290
I literally just copied
and pasted it over

00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:35.890
into a directory in
my blogdown site.

00:38:35.890 --> 00:38:39.800
And then I also used [INAUDIBLE]
slides to make my presentation

00:38:39.800 --> 00:38:41.120
slides in R Markdown.

00:38:41.120 --> 00:38:43.130
So you can see
that I also copied

00:38:43.130 --> 00:38:45.500
and pasted those directly
from my R Markdown

00:38:45.500 --> 00:38:49.460
site and directory
in my blogdown site.

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:52.460
And then this allows me to take
advantage of one of the best

00:38:52.460 --> 00:38:54.900
benefits of using
blogdown for teaching,

00:38:54.900 --> 00:38:58.460
which is that when I use
the add-in to build my site,

00:38:58.460 --> 00:39:01.910
so I use the blogdown
add-in Serve Site,

00:39:01.910 --> 00:39:05.990
it creates a folder
called _public.

00:39:05.990 --> 00:39:07.632
And that's the
website that I see.

00:39:07.632 --> 00:39:09.590
But what it also does
under the hood for me is,

00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:13.820
it builds any other kind of R
Markdown content that I have.

00:39:13.820 --> 00:39:17.450
So I can house my other
R Markdown HTML documents

00:39:17.450 --> 00:39:19.622
in here, as well as
my [INAUDIBLE] slides.

00:39:19.622 --> 00:39:21.080
I can do it all,
and I can build it

00:39:21.080 --> 00:39:25.580
all with one button,
which is really nice.

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:27.640
So just a quick tour
of some of the files

00:39:27.640 --> 00:39:29.230
in a blogdown project.

00:39:29.230 --> 00:39:31.180
The content folder
is probably the place

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:33.250
that you'll want to
dig into the most.

00:39:33.250 --> 00:39:35.380
Your primary R Markdown
files will live here.

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:38.145
You can also use
plain Markdown files.

00:39:38.145 --> 00:39:40.990
The YAML does depend on the
theme that you choose, though.

00:39:40.990 --> 00:39:44.020
So this means that you'll need
to change any existing YAML

00:39:44.020 --> 00:39:45.400
to adapt to your theme.

00:39:45.400 --> 00:39:46.900
And this is also
how you can kind of

00:39:46.900 --> 00:39:48.160
get locked into a theme.

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:52.840
So once you choose a theme in
blogdown, it's tough to switch.

00:39:52.840 --> 00:39:55.960
Sometimes I also stash and
build other R Markdown documents

00:39:55.960 --> 00:39:57.840
in the static folder.

00:39:57.840 --> 00:40:00.970
And if you go to the repository
associated with my demo site,

00:40:00.970 --> 00:40:04.690
you can see how I did that
to deploy my R Markdown

00:40:04.690 --> 00:40:06.922
labs and my [INAUDIBLE] slides.

00:40:06.922 --> 00:40:08.380
So that's just a
taste of blogdown,

00:40:08.380 --> 00:40:12.020
to give you a flavor for the
things that are possible.

00:40:12.020 --> 00:40:14.270
So the good parts of blog
down is that you can finally

00:40:14.270 --> 00:40:15.260
now have subfolders.

00:40:15.260 --> 00:40:18.038
You can have a content
folder organization

00:40:18.038 --> 00:40:19.580
that might help you
if you have a lot

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:24.350
of different things like data,
assignments, labs, slides.

00:40:24.350 --> 00:40:25.520
You can also knit anything.

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:26.540
So you can have--

00:40:26.540 --> 00:40:29.482
different output
formats are possible.

00:40:29.482 --> 00:40:31.190
blogdown's also built
on top of bookdown.

00:40:31.190 --> 00:40:34.770
So any of the features that are
present in the bookdown HTML

00:40:34.770 --> 00:40:38.330
format are also
possible in blogdown.

00:40:38.330 --> 00:40:41.540
The bad things, in my
experience, are Hugo.

00:40:41.540 --> 00:40:43.610
Hugo is a new dependency
that we've added all.

00:40:43.610 --> 00:40:46.270
The other options that
we've discussed so far,

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:48.710
R Markdown sites,
Distill, and bookdown,

00:40:48.710 --> 00:40:50.930
only had an R package
that they depended on.

00:40:50.930 --> 00:40:53.390
But now when you
work with blogdown,

00:40:53.390 --> 00:40:57.590
you are depending on installing
Hugo, which is a language that

00:40:57.590 --> 00:40:59.120
does have different versions.

00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:00.350
It requires updates.

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:02.550
There's a bit of
maintenance involved.

00:41:02.550 --> 00:41:04.610
There's also a more complex
directory structure ,

00:41:04.610 --> 00:41:06.920
so with being able
to have subfolders,

00:41:06.920 --> 00:41:09.680
you also gain more
complexity as well.

00:41:09.680 --> 00:41:11.210
So I wouldn't
necessarily recommend

00:41:11.210 --> 00:41:13.310
this for short notice,
but I do think it's

00:41:13.310 --> 00:41:16.220
a nice longer-term solution.

00:41:16.220 --> 00:41:17.870
Some general blogdown advice--

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:21.360
I do not recommend that you
update Hugo during your course

00:41:21.360 --> 00:41:23.240
if you're using blogdown.

00:41:23.240 --> 00:41:25.610
I also do not recommend
that you update your Hugo

00:41:25.610 --> 00:41:28.540
theme during your course.

00:41:28.540 --> 00:41:30.760
And I also just
generally recommend,

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:33.250
don't update anything
during your course,

00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:36.260
based on personal experience.

00:41:36.260 --> 00:41:38.800
So how do you share
your blogdown site?

00:41:38.800 --> 00:41:44.440
The way you do that is by--
let's see if this play--

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:47.950
is by taking that public folder
and dragging and dropping that

00:41:47.950 --> 00:41:51.160
into the app.netlify.com/drop.

00:41:51.160 --> 00:41:53.740
You can see that it's
thinking, thinking, thinking,

00:41:53.740 --> 00:41:56.260
and I get a very nice
random domain name,

00:41:56.260 --> 00:42:00.070
[? nostalgic-brahoomtheta. ?]
So that would probably be tough

00:42:00.070 --> 00:42:01.087
for me to remember.

00:42:01.087 --> 00:42:02.920
I could use the trick
that we showed earlier

00:42:02.920 --> 00:42:05.050
to change that custom
domain name to something

00:42:05.050 --> 00:42:06.970
more memorable.

00:42:06.970 --> 00:42:10.430
And here's a shortcut
that you can use as well.

00:42:10.430 --> 00:42:12.970
So if you go to that
repository that I made,

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:17.590
the share-blogdown repository,
I created a little button called

00:42:17.590 --> 00:42:18.865
a Deploy to Netlify button.

00:42:18.865 --> 00:42:20.740
Now, this works if you're
logged into GitHub.

00:42:20.740 --> 00:42:23.230
So if you're already using
GitHub for version control,

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:26.200
if you click on that
Deploy to Netlify button,

00:42:26.200 --> 00:42:27.670
you can see that
what it does is,

00:42:27.670 --> 00:42:30.880
it allows you to take the
same site that I'm showing you

00:42:30.880 --> 00:42:33.010
in my repository.

00:42:33.010 --> 00:42:36.730
You can then link it up
to your Netlify account.

00:42:36.730 --> 00:42:40.570
So here, Desiree's showing
logging into her existing

00:42:40.570 --> 00:42:41.412
Netlify account.

00:42:41.412 --> 00:42:43.120
DESIREE DE LEON:
Existing GitHub account.

00:42:43.120 --> 00:42:44.860
ALISON HILL: GitHub account.

00:42:44.860 --> 00:42:46.770
Thank you.

00:42:46.770 --> 00:42:50.280
And it creates a new
repository in her GitHub

00:42:50.280 --> 00:42:52.800
with the exact same
files that I had.

00:42:52.800 --> 00:42:54.963
And it automatically
deploys it to Netlify

00:42:54.963 --> 00:42:56.880
with all the same settings
that I had created.

00:42:56.880 --> 00:43:00.210
So now Desiree has her own
copy of my exact same site.

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:02.270
With one button, she
clicked on the Deploy

00:43:02.270 --> 00:43:04.770
to Netlify button
in my repository.

00:43:04.770 --> 00:43:06.480
Her site deploy is in progress.

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:10.260
It takes a little while
for Hugo to build the site.

00:43:10.260 --> 00:43:14.430
So insert Jeopardy! music
here while it's going.

00:43:14.430 --> 00:43:15.020
There we go.

00:43:15.020 --> 00:43:17.460
Stoic-snyder is now live.

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:18.540
So she can click on that.

00:43:18.540 --> 00:43:21.020
So that's the exact same
site that I just showed you,

00:43:21.020 --> 00:43:23.730
but now it's based off
source files in her GitHub

00:43:23.730 --> 00:43:28.740
repository deployed to her own
URL in Netlify, stoic-snyder.

00:43:28.740 --> 00:43:31.590
And then she can change
that, she can edit the files,

00:43:31.590 --> 00:43:34.680
and she can employ
continuous deployment

00:43:34.680 --> 00:43:37.650
with Netlify, which is by
connecting your GitHub account

00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:40.140
and using GitHub to
store your source files.

00:43:40.140 --> 00:43:42.390
You can connect that to
Netlify so that every time you

00:43:42.390 --> 00:43:44.670
make a change and
you push to GitHub,

00:43:44.670 --> 00:43:47.170
the website can
automatically update.

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:49.620
So this is how I would
really recommend--

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:52.260
if you want to use blogdown,
it's one of the great benefits

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:57.120
without needing to worry
too much about remembering

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:58.600
to update your site.

00:43:58.600 --> 00:44:01.800
So if you're familiar with Git
and GitHub for version control,

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:05.550
and you'd like to take a foray
into blogdown I recommend that.

00:44:05.550 --> 00:44:07.560
We also listed
some more resources

00:44:07.560 --> 00:44:10.080
here at our GitHub
repository for this talk.

00:44:10.080 --> 00:44:12.710
It's at rstudio-education.

00:44:12.710 --> 00:44:14.392
It's called Sharing
Short Notice.

00:44:14.392 --> 00:44:16.350
If you look in the GitHub
issues there as well,

00:44:16.350 --> 00:44:19.950
I made a list of a bunch of
different educational websites,

00:44:19.950 --> 00:44:22.220
course websites, using
the different tools.

00:44:22.220 --> 00:44:25.440
So I've tagged them whether
they use blogdown, R Markdown,

00:44:25.440 --> 00:44:26.410
bookdown, et cetera.

00:44:26.410 --> 00:44:29.520
So you can see links to
the source deployed version

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:30.760
and to the repository.

00:44:30.760 --> 00:44:33.880
So you can see the
source files as well.

00:44:33.880 --> 00:44:35.857
So thank you for
joining us today.

00:44:35.857 --> 00:44:37.440
We hope we've left
